WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:09.755
How does Google store the entire internet, on drives that fail every day, and still search it in milliseconds?

00:00:09.755 --> 00:00:17.511
A distributed file system: split each file into chunks, replicate each across cheap servers, and give clients one namespace.

00:00:17.511 --> 00:00:25.053
All four share the same chunks-and-replicas substrate; what evolved is how the metadata is coordinated.

00:00:25.053 --> 00:00:32.595
GFS, two thousand three: a single master holds all the metadata, the bottleneck every later system attacks.

00:00:32.595 --> 00:00:40.136
HDFS, two thousand six: the same design, open-sourced.

00:00:40.136 --> 00:00:47.678
S3, also two thousand six: no master at all, leaderless metadata, elastic, and strongly consistent since twenty twenty.

00:00:47.678 --> 00:00:55.220
Colossus, twenty-ten on: Google shards the metadata across many servers, breaking the bottleneck.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:01:01.025
Colossus also drops three-way replication for something cheaper: Reed-Solomon erasure coding.

00:01:01.025 --> 00:01:06.830
Take six data blocks, compute three parity blocks from them, and store all nine.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:12.635
Lose any three, and the math rebuilds them from the six that survive.

00:01:12.635 --> 00:01:18.440
That tolerates three failures at fifty percent overhead, where three full copies cost two hundred percent.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:24.322
Side by side, the four differ on one axis: master versus leaderless, and the consistency and scale that follow.

00:01:24.322 --> 00:01:30.205
Strong consistency for transactional state; eventual consistency for blob storage at planet scale.

00:01:30.205 --> 00:01:36.087
Pick the one that fits your access pattern.

00:01:36.087 --> 00:01:41.490
Distributed file systems are the foundation under almost every cloud system you use.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:46.892
Photos in iCloud, videos on YouTube, logs in DataDog, all the same chunks-with-replication pattern.

00:01:46.892 --> 00:01:52.295
The variations are which consistency, leadership, and scaling you need.
